{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>רַב, עוֹד יוֹסֵף בְּנִי חָי.</b> Since the Holy One, blessed be He, promised him that if not one of his sons would die during his lifetime he would not see the face of Gehinnom, and he would merit what is said (Psalms 31:20) ״מָה רַב טוּבְךָ אֲשֶׁר צָפַנְתָּ לִּירֵאֶיךָ״, therefore he said here ״רַב״ — from now on I shall merit the reward of the World to Come, which is called ״רַב״, for it is a world that is entirely long, unlike the life of this world, which is little and evil, for ״לֹא הַרְבֵּה יִזְכֹּר יְמֵי חַיָּיו.״ And furthermore, that he had already received the punishment for neglecting the commandment of honoring his father in this world, and from now on he would receive his reward complete in the World to Come. Therefore it says ״וַיִּזְבַּח זְבָחִים לֵאלֹהֵי אָבִיו יִצְחָק״, and he did not mention also Abraham, but rather he wished to repair what he had damaged and returned to conduct himself with honor toward his father Isaac. Another thing: ״רַב, עוֹד יוֹסֵף בְּנִי חָי״ — why is it that you say that he rules over all the land of Egypt? רַב — it is enough for me that he is alive; it is enough for me to have contentment of spirit in this, and I do not care whether he rules or not."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The phrase לֹא הַרְבֵּה יִזְכֹּר יְמֵי חַיָּיו appears to be an unmarked biblical quotation from Ecclesiastes 7:15 (לֹא הַרְבֵּה יִזְכֹּר יְמֵי חַיָּיו); rendered with quotation marks accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:38.871639648Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}